{"title":"Nordwind E-Bikes","sub":"Should a Danish premium e-bike maker enter the US?","type":"Market entry","industry":"Consumer durables / mobility","geo":"Denmark → United States","diff":2,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"35 min","skills":"Market sizing · breakeven · entry mode","prompt":"Nordwind makes premium e-bikes in Denmark and sells them through 600 bike shops across Europe (revenue $150M). European growth is slowing, so the founders are considering the United States. Their goal: $44M of US revenue (measured at Nordwind's selling price to shops) within five years, and an operating profit in the US by year 3. Should they enter, and if so, how?","clarify":[{"q":"Product?","a":"Premium city and trekking e-bikes, average retail price about $4,000."},{"q":"How do they sell?","a":"Through independent bike shops. Nordwind sells to shops at 60% of retail price."},{"q":"How is the revenue goal measured?","a":"At Nordwind's selling price to shops, not at retail prices."},{"q":"Any US presence?","a":"None today. A few US shops import small volumes informally."}],"structure":{"root":"Should Nordwind enter the US, and how?","branches":[{"head":"Is the market attractive?","items":["Size & growth of US e-bikes","Premium segment share","Margins, competitors, regulation & tariffs"]},{"head":"Can we win?","items":["Product edge vs US brands","Brand awareness (zero today)","Access to bike shops"]},{"head":"Do the economics work?","items":["Volume needed for $44M","Unit contribution","Fixed costs, breakeven, payback"]},{"head":"How to enter?","items":["Distributor vs own network vs acquisition","Pilot regions","Timing & risks"]}]},"hypothesis":"The US market is big enough. The real question is whether Nordwind can reach enough shops fast enough, so I expect the entry mode to decide the case.","questions":[{"q":"Here is some market data. What market share would Nordwind need to hit its goal in year 5?","exhibit":{"kind":"table","headers":["US e-bike market","Value"],"rows":[["Units sold, year 1","1.0M"],["Annual growth","10%"],["Premium segment (>$3,000) share of units","15%"],["Nordwind price to shops","$2,400 (60% of $4,000)"]],"title":"Exhibit 1 · US market facts (case data)","note":null,"align":["l","r"],"boldLast":false},"answer":"<p>Year-5 market = 1.0M × 1.1⁴ ≈ 1.46M units → premium = 15% ≈ <b>220k units</b>.</p> <p>Units needed = $44M ÷ $2,400 ≈ <b>18,300 bikes</b> → share of premium ≈ 18.3k ÷ 220k ≈ <b>8%</b>.</p> <p><b>So-what:</b> 8% would make Nordwind roughly the #4 premium brand within five years. That's ambitious but not crazy in a fragmented segment. Next I'd look at the competition.</p>"},{"q":"Here are the premium competitors. What does this tell you?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"367pt\" height=\"159.66pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 367 159.66\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\">\n <metadata>\n  <rdf:RDF xmlns:dc=\"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/\" xmlns:cc=\"http://creativecommons.org/ns#\" xmlns:rdf=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#\">\n   <cc:Work>\n    <dc:type rdf:resource=\"http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/StillImage\"/>\n    <dc:date>2026-11-06T21:01:25.128191</dc:date>\n    <dc:format>image/svg+xml</dc:format>\n    <dc:creator>\n     <cc:Agent>\n      <dc:title>Matplotlib v3.11.2, https://matplotlib.org/</dc:title>\n     </cc:Agent>\n    </dc:creator>\n   </cc:Work>\n  </rdf:RDF>\n </metadata>\n <defs>\n  <style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style>\n </defs>\n <g id=\"figure_1\">\n  <g id=\"patch_1\">\n   <path d=\"M 0 159.66 \nL 367 159.66 \nL 367 -0 \nL 0 -0 \nL 0 159.66 \nz\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/>\n  </g>\n  <g id=\"axes_1\">\n   <g id=\"patch_2\">\n    <path d=\"M 25 133.76 \nL 359.8 133.76 \nL 359.8 11.792 \nL 25 11.792 \nL 25 133.76 \nz\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/>\n   </g>\n   <g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\">\n    <g id=\"xtick_1\">\n     <g id=\"line2d_1\">\n      <defs>\n       <path id=\"m3abf2f