1920 2573 \nQ 2336 2573 2557 2755 \nQ 2778 2938 2778 3277 \nL 2778 3373 \nQ 2778 3712 2557 3894 \nQ 2336 4077 1920 4077 \nQ 1504 4077 1283 3894 \nQ 1062 3712 1062 3373 \nL 1062 3277 \nQ 1062 2938 1283 2755 \nQ 1504 2573 1920 2573 \nz\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"/>\n     </defs>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-42\"/>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-7b\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n   <g id=\"text_15\">\n    <!-- 45% -->\n    <g style=\"fill: #2d3748\" transform=\"translate(310.710057 15.4) scale(0.08 -0.08)\">\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3e\"/>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-3f\" transform=\"translate(60 0)\"/>\n     <use xlink:href=\"#IBMPlexSans-Regular-7b\" transform=\"translate(120 0)\"/>\n    </g>\n   </g>\n  </g>\n </g>\n <defs>\n  <clipPath id=\"p24415bfab2\">\n   <rect x=\"25\" y=\"11.792\" width=\"334.8\" height=\"121.968\"/>\n  </clipPath>\n </defs>\n</svg>\n","title":"Exhibit 2 · US premium e-bike share of units, %","note":null},"answer":"<p>The market is <b>fragmented</b>: the top four hold 55% and a long tail holds 45%. A European brand (B) already holds 15%, so US buyers clearly accept European premium bikes. To reach 8%, Nordwind needs to match Brand D. <b>So-what:</b> the share target is realistic if Nordwind gets its bikes into enough shops. That makes the route to market the key decision.</p>"},{"q":"Nordwind is choosing between two ways to enter. Which is better?","exhibit":{"kind":"table","headers":["","A · US distributor","B · Own sales network"],"rows":[["Upfront investment","None","$12M (team, warehouse, launch)"],["Annual fixed cost","$1.5M (marketing)","$6M"],["Distributor fee","15% of price to shops","—"],["Variable cost per bike (make, ship, duty)","$1,500","$1,500"]],"title":"Exhibit 3 · Entry options","note":null,"align":["l","r","r"],"boldLast":false},"answer":"<p><b>Contribution per bike:</b> A = $2,400 × 85% − $1,500 = <b>$540</b>; B = $2,400 − $1,500 = <b>$900</b>.</p> <p><b>Annual profit is the same when</b> 540V − 1.5M = 900V − 6M → 360V = 4.5M → <b>V = 12,500 bikes/year</b>.</p> <p>Below 12,500 bikes a year the distributor is better; above it, the own network wins. At year-5 volume (~18,300): A ≈ $8.4M, B ≈ $10.5M.</p> <p>Own-network breakeven = $6M ÷ $900 ≈ 6,700 bikes/year. If volumes ramp 3k → 7k → 11k → 15k → 18.3k, B makes a small profit in year 2, but recovering the $12M upfront takes until year 5.</p>","note":"Let the candidate set up the indifference equation. Good candidates realise the answer is 'distributor first, then switch'."},{"q":"What risks should the founders weigh?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<ul><li><b>Tariffs and trade policy</b> on EU-made goods can wipe out the $900 margin overnight. Consider US final assembly.</li> <li><b>Distributor lock-in</b>: a 5-year exclusive contract would block the switch to an own network.</li> <li><b>Brand awareness</b>: no US brand recognition, so early volumes may be slower.</li> <li><b>Service</b>: e-bikes need repairs and spare parts; shops need training.</li></ul>"}],"reco":"Yes, enter the US, but through a distributor first. Hitting $44M needs about 8% of the premium segment, which is achievable in a fragmented market. A distributor is more profitable until volumes pass ~12,500 bikes a year and needs no $12M upfront, so it also meets the 'profitable by year 3' goal more safely. The contract should be 3 years or less, with the right to take over distribution once that volume is reached. Main risk: tariffs. As a next step, model US assembly and start in 2-3 bike-friendly states.","great":["States the 8% share and judges whether it's realistic","Sets up the 12,500-bike crossover clearly","Recommends a sequence (distributor → own network), not just one option","Raises tariffs without prompting"],"id":2,"slug":"nordwind-e-bikes"}